# The message of the day for each University of Nottingham hall of residence a
# host can be named after. `flake.halls` in flake/parts/hosts.nix publishes this
# table and a host record reads its own entry, so the keys are the host names,
# which are the file names under `hosts/`.
{
  ancaster = ''
    Ancaster Hall is named after a Lincolnshire village most people have driven
    past without stopping. Its blocks are named after Lincolnshire places too,
    which makes the theme unusually coherent for a university hall. It houses
    around 270 students. In 1970 it became one of the University of
    Nottingham's first mixed-sex halls, and its first Warden, Dr Virginia van
    der Lande, spent the next sixteen years managing that with considerable
    energy. Two gilded herons guard the wrought iron gates. Whether they mean
    aspiration or warning is left as an exercise for the incoming fresher.'';

  bonington = ''
    Bonington Student Village occupies a campus built in 1915 for an
    agricultural college. The War Office requisitioned it at once and used it
    to hold up to 500 German officers who had already failed to escape from
    other camps. In September 1917 twenty-two of them escaped through a
    forty-yard tunnel. They had dug it with a jam pot and a piece of bent zinc
    and hidden fifteen tonnes of soil under a lecture room. Most were
    recaptured within hours. One was found blackberrying, and another asked
    directions to the nearest railway station. The campus joined the
    University of Nottingham in 1947. The barbed wire has since given way to
    around 650 student beds and a bar called The Barn.'';

  cavendish = ''
    Cavendish Hall was built in 1964 to house 161 women. It is named after the
    Cavendish family, Dukes of Devonshire, whose ancestral seat is in
    Derbyshire. That makes the connection to a Nottingham halls corridor only
    marginally less tenuous than the grass moat separating Cavendish from the
    rest of campus. The hall surrounds a rose garden quadrangle, which suggests
    that someone, at some point, had aspirations. It is now mixed-sex and
    catered, with around 284 students, most of whom will never notice the rose
    garden.'';

  cripps = ''
    Cripps Hall is a Grade II listed building, one of fewer than twenty
    post-war structures thought significant enough to protect. That is either a
    mark of McMorran and Whitby's architectural distinction or a sign that the
    listing authorities had a productive afternoon in 1988. It is built around
    traditional quads, with a wood-panelled dining hall and a bell tower that
    does in fact work. It is the largest catered hall on University Park, and
    has been co-educational since 2000, a change that presumably took some
    adjustment. The bar does not serve alcohol, so residents who want a drink
    walk to Hugh Stewart Hall. The journey is a character-building exercise in
    putting principle aside.'';

  derby = ''
    Derby Hall was completed in 1963 to a stripped-down classical design by the
    New Zealand architect Brian O'Rorke, whose better known work is the
    interiors of ocean liners. That may explain the institutional catering. It
    is arranged around a central quad, with a later extension called Matlock,
    and houses around 334 students. All of them will become personally
    acquainted with the fire alarm at 3am, courtesy of someone's toast. The
    pantry kitchen contains a toaster, a microwave and an ironing board, which
    suggests the architects had a very particular vision of student life.'';

  florence = ''
    Florence Boot Hall opened in 1928, funded and furnished by Florence Boot.
    Her husband Jesse Boot gave the university the land the whole campus is
    built on, so the family had form for this sort of thing. Florence invented
    the Boots Book Lovers' Library, which at its peak had half a million
    subscribers, and is arguably more interesting than the hall named after
    her. A £15m refurbishment finished in 2023 and brought the building, at
    last, into the era of functional heating.'';

  hugh-stewart = ''
    Hugh Stewart Hall occupies a pleasantly wooded corner of University Park.
    Its 340 beds are spread across twelve blocks, each named after somewhere in
    Lincolnshire, a county not otherwise known for inspiring people. F.E.
    Wooley designed it and it opened in 1962. It calls itself the largest and
    most traditional catered hall, and upheld the second half of that with some
    vigour by remaining all-male until 1997. Until 2006 it also had the last
    student-run bar on campus, which the university then apparently decided was
    quite enough of that.'';

  lenton-wortley = ''
    Lenton and Wortley Hall is named after a district of Nottingham and after
    the last Principal of University College Nottingham, whose principal
    achievement was being the last one. It occupies the quieter northern end of
    campus, where, according to student lore, nobody ever meets its residents
    anywhere else. The crest features a squirrel, which feels about right. It
    began as two separate halls, merged some time in the 1980s, presumably
    because one of them was not doing enough on its own.'';

  lincoln = ''
    Lincoln Hall opened in 1962 with a gatehouse and a turret, which is more
    fortification than a hall of residence needs. It stayed single-sex until
    1997, which at least explains the turret. It has since reinvented itself as
    a Quiet Zone, where alcohol is discouraged and reasonable bedtimes are not,
    and is arguably the only hall in Britain a politician would be proud to
    have lived in. Its most notable JCR President became MP for South Holland
    and the Deepings, a constituency that sounds exactly like somewhere a quiet
    zone would be.'';

  melton = ''
    Melton Hall occupies the crescent-shaped postgraduate end of Jubilee
    Campus. The site was a bicycle factory until 1999, and the architecture
    does nothing to discourage you from pondering that. Sir Michael Hopkins
    designed it, and it is named after Melton Mowbray, a Leicestershire market
    town better known for pork pies than for academia. It is the university's
    one concession to housing people with master's degrees: 149 en-suite rooms,
    with kitchens shared between up to thirteen adults who presumably know
    better than to leave their washing-up. Rooms facing the car park are
    reportedly larger than those facing the lake, which tells you something
    about the priorities of the crescent's geometry, if not about the
    architect.'';

  newark = ''
    Newark Hall is named after a Nottinghamshire market town whose civic motto,
    adopted in 1912 to commemorate a Civil War siege, translates loosely as
    "Trust in God and Sally Forth". The same spirit of mild defiance suits the
    second-largest hall on a campus whose library is an inverted cone on an
    island in an artificial lake. Sir Michael Hopkins designed Newark Hall in
    the shape of a figure-of-eight. Its 400 or so students eat in a restaurant
    shared with the residents of Southwell Hall rather than in a dining room of
    their own, a democratic arrangement that presumably builds character. King
    John died of dysentery in the original Newark in 1216. Conditions here are
    understood to be somewhat improved.'';

  nightingale = ''
    Nightingale is the smallest hall on University Park and has been quietly
    compensating for its 157 beds since 1950. It went mixed in 2000 and gained
    three-quarter beds and mini fridges in 2007, both of which are now
    unremarkable. Its first warden, Audrey Beecham, was a poet and a historian,
    and is reportedly still in residence: she is said to haunt the building.
    For a hall this small that is less a ghost story than a space problem. The
    lift has a button for a fifth floor, but there are no student rooms on the
    fifth floor. The university has not commented.'';

  rutland = ''
    Rutland Hall takes its name from England's smallest county, a place so
    modest that it was removed from official maps in 1974 and had to campaign
    to be reinstated in 1997, which it duly was. The hall opened in 1964 as a
    men-only institution and admitted women from 1970. It was comprehensively
    refurbished in 1999. Its most distinctive feature is an octagonal library,
    either a charming quirk or a space-efficiency puzzle, depending on how many
    right angles you feel a bookshelf deserves.'';

  sherwood = ''
    J. Fletcher Watson built Sherwood Hall in 1963 and 1964 as an
    interpretation of the traditional collegiate quadrangle in "contemporary
    forms and materials". In practice that meant white shiplap weatherboarding,
    which gives the whole thing a faint air of New England rather than
    Nottinghamshire. It is named after Sherwood Forest, though the launderette
    tempers any romantic associations with Robin Hood. It was originally
    all-male, a tradition since quietly retired along with, one hopes, most of
    the attitudes that went with it.'';

  southwell = ''
    Southwell Hall takes its name from a small Nottinghamshire market town best
    known for a medieval minster that historians describe, without apparent
    irony, as a "hidden gem". That is perhaps what you call something that
    lacks the pulling power of, say, York. The hall stands on the site of the
    former Raleigh Bicycle Company factory. The university commemorates this
    with a metallic bicycle sculpture and a studied silence about production
    having moved abroad before the site was sold. With 205 en-suite rooms and a
    dining arrangement shared with Newark Hall, Southwell offers the particular
    camaraderie of students who cross a car park to eat breakfast in another
    building's company.'';

  willoughby = ''
    Willoughby Hall is named after the Willoughby family. Sir Francis
    Willoughby built nearby Wollaton Hall in 1580, largely on the proceeds of
    coal mining, a venture that went on to bankrupt him. The university
    continued the tradition of ambitious construction on a strained budget by
    putting up Willoughby Hall in reinforced concrete in 1964, the first hall
    on campus built that way, presumably because nobody had tried to stop them.
    Wollaton Hall has since served as Wayne Manor in a Batman film, which the
    family almost certainly did not foresee when they named themselves after a
    village in Nottinghamshire.'';
}
